A context follows a slot. A provider written in a component's body covers the
content a caller writes into that component's slots, because that content is
rendered where the body inserts it -- so `list { item() }` puts the item under
whatever `list`'s body wrapped its slot in, though the item is written at the
call site.

Everywhere else this pass threads a context by *lexical* position, and
lexically a slot population is the caller's, outside the callee's provider. So
the values a call site supplies come from a table recorded per component and
per slot before any body is lowered (`computeSlotEnvs`), composed down the
provider chain in the callee's own terms and substituted with what the call
site threaded to that callee.

The nesting is the assertion: the outer item is 1 because `myList`'s body
provides `depth + 1`, and the inner item is 2 because the inner list is itself
inside the outer item's content slot, so it reads 1 and provides 2. One is the
slot crossing and the other is the accumulation on top of it.

deny * `\bdepth\b` -- the context name is not a symbol any target declares
-- slots.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

context #depth(0)

component myList(items ...component) node {
    depth(depth + 1) {
        vbox {
            items
        }
    }
}

component myItem(content ...component) node {
    hbox {
        text(value="d={depth}")
        vbox {
            content
        }
    }
}

window(title="t") {
    myList {
        myItem {
            myList {
                myItem {}
            }
        }
    }
}
